1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,983
ReleaseGuard helps an engineering lead answer a practical question: can this release move forward,

2
00:00:04,983 --> 00:00:07,118
based on the evidence we have?

3
00:00:07,118 --> 00:00:11,791
A Checkout change can depend on Payments, which depends on Ledger. An incident in

4
00:00:11,791 --> 00:00:16,130
a connected service can block the release even if Checkout itself looks healthy.

5
00:00:16,130 --> 00:00:22,871
This demonstration uses a fictional company and synthetic incident records. The database queries, Gemini

6
00:00:22,871 --> 00:00:26,723
requests, saved reviews, and owner-assigned tasks are real.

7
00:00:26,723 --> 00:00:32,466
The service map follows relationships returned by FalkorDB. Selecting a service reveals its sources.

8
00:00:32,466 --> 00:00:38,209
Source records include their version and checksum, so an explanation can be checked against

9
00:00:38,209 --> 00:00:39,029
the evidence.

10
00:00:39,029 --> 00:00:45,243
Memory also matters. In Past fixes, increasing retries previously worsened overload. A timeout and

11
00:00:45,243 --> 00:00:51,457
circuit breaker procedure was associated with recovery, but still needs validation for the current

12
00:00:51,457 --> 00:00:51,901
incident.

13
00:00:51,901 --> 00:00:57,049
Here is a recorded change to the evidence. A new blocking incident is added

14
00:00:57,049 --> 00:01:02,197
to Ledger. The previous Ready recommendation immediately becomes Review outdated. The agent cannot keep

15
00:01:02,197 --> 00:01:04,403
treating its old answer as current.

16
00:01:04,403 --> 00:01:09,846
A fresh review runs in a background worker. Gemini chooses bounded source and history

17
00:01:09,846 --> 00:01:15,288
tools, saves structured findings, and hands them to a separate verifier. Deterministic evidence and

18
00:01:15,288 --> 00:01:17,620
freshness checks gate the final action.

19
00:01:17,620 --> 00:01:23,083
This section uses separate recorded takes, and waiting time is shortened. It does not

20
00:01:23,083 --> 00:01:25,033
imply an uninterrupted live recording.

21
00:01:25,033 --> 00:01:30,284
The completed review found the path Checkout, Payments, Ledger, and incident thirteen. The release

22
00:01:30,284 --> 00:01:35,535
became Blocked, and a task was persisted for Money, the recorded owner. It took

23
00:01:35,535 --> 00:01:38,160
four model requests and six tool calls.

24
00:01:38,160 --> 00:01:43,947
The successful cloud run took about seventy seconds. About fifty seconds were model and

25
00:01:43,947 --> 00:01:48,493
API time. These are individual measured samples, not a speedup claim.

26
00:01:48,493 --> 00:01:53,644
Fifty backend tests and eleven browser checks passed. A fair same-facts adjacency-list baseline agreed

27
00:01:53,644 --> 00:01:58,794
with the graph on all four small relationship cases and was faster in memory.

28
00:01:58,794 --> 00:02:03,209
We do not claim graph accuracy or speed superiority on this dataset.

29
00:02:03,209 --> 00:02:09,249
FalkorDB is central to the connected evidence, queries, saved agent handoff, tasks, and freshness

30
00:02:09,249 --> 00:02:14,857
checks. If it is unavailable, the application cannot confirm a current release decision.

31
00:02:14,857 --> 00:02:21,718
ReleaseGuard does not deploy software. It has no authenticated multi-tenant API, high availability, or

32
00:02:21,718 --> 00:02:26,619
proven disaster recovery. Provider failures and unsupported findings withhold actions.

33
00:02:26,619 --> 00:02:32,110
The result is a traceable release investigation: connected evidence, useful memory, an accountable owner,

34
00:02:32,110 --> 00:02:37,601
and a recommendation that knows when it is outdated. AI assistance was used for

35
00:02:37,601 --> 00:02:39,170
implementation and this narration.
